<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallipoli campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Dardanelles campaign · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> campaign was an Allied attempt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> to force the Dardanelles and knock the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> out of the war. British, French, Australian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> troops landed on the Gallipoli peninsula in April 1915 but were held by Ottoman forces, including those under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>, and withdrew by January 1916 after heavy losses. The landings became a founding national memory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and New Zealand, marked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anzac-day">Anzac Day</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallipoli-campaign.jpg" alt="Gallipoli campaign" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LoudHmen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G.C._18_March_1915_Gallipoli_Campaign_Article.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on 31 October 1914 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> raid. Allied planners wanted to take control of the Turkish straits, expose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to bombardment by warships and cut it off from the Asian part of the empire. Success could have opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> and Dardanelles to Allied supplies for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and given Western control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>. The campaign began on 19 February 1915 with an Anglo-French bombardment of the Dardanelles forts. On 18 March the Entente fleet of 18 battleships tried to force the narrowest point. The French battleship Bouvet struck a mine and capsized in two minutes, and the battleships Irresistible and Ocean were also lost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After the naval failure, General Sir Ian Hamilton was appointed to command 78,000 men of the Mediterranean Expeditionary Force. Landings were made on 25 April 1915 at five beaches. The 29th Division landed at Cape Helles, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australian-and-new-zealand-army-corps">Australian and New Zealand Army Corps</a> landed north of Gaba Tepe at a place that became known as ANZAC. The French made a diversion at Kum Kale. The Ottoman 5th Army, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> force commanded by Sanders, held the high ground, and Lieutenant Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> had predicted the likely landing areas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> were checked at Krithia in three battles, and trench warfare followed.</p><p>A force of 42,000 Ottoman troops attacked Anzac on 19 May and suffered about 13,000 casualties. In August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> tried a new offensive at Sari Bair and Suvla, which failed. Criticism of Hamilton's performance was smuggled out by journalists, and he was replaced by Sir Charles Monro. Autumn and winter brought gales, blizzards and flooding. The Allies evacuated the peninsula, and the campaign ended on 9 January 1916.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Casualty figures vary. Edward J. Erickson wrote in 2001 that over 100,000 men were killed, including 56,000 to 68,000 Ottoman soldiers and around 53,000 British and French. Fisher resigned in May 1915, and the crisis led H. H. Asquith to form a coalition government with the Conservatives. Churchill was demoted from First Lord of the Admiralty, and the Dardanelles Commission investigated the failure.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> the campaign is regarded as a defining moment, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a>, who rose to prominence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, became the first president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> in 1923. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> it is regarded as a baptism of fire, linked to the emergence of a national identity and the Anzac spirit, and 25 April is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anzac-day">Anzac Day</a>. About 50,000 Australians and 16,000 to 17,000 New Zealanders served there. Analysis of the campaign led many to believe that amphibious assaults could not succeed against modern defences.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gallipoli campaign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the First World War more than a million Indians served overseas in France, Mesopotamia, <b>Gallipoli</b>, Palestine and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The landing of the Australian and New Zealand Army Corps at <b>Gallipoli</b> on 25 April 1915, commemorated as Anzac Day, became a founding national legend, and the Australian Corps under Sir John Monash fought with distinction on the Western Front in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>British troops left Cape Helles, ending the <b>Gallipoli campaign</b> in an Ottoman victory.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>William Massey's Reform government held office from 1912 to 1925 and sent the expeditionary force whose losses at <b>Gallipoli</b> in 1915 shaped national identity.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-new-zealand">Dominion of New Zealand</a></li></ul></section>
